<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalinago</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Island Caribs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kalinago, also known as the Island Caribs, are an Indigenous people of the Lesser Antilles, who lived on islands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a>, Saint Vincent, Guadeloupe and Martinique when Europeans arrived. They were skilled seafarers who fought fiercely against Spanish, French and English colonists. Wars, disease and displacement greatly reduced them, and their main surviving community lives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago-territory">Kalinago Territory</a> on Dominica.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalinago.jpg" alt="Kalinago" width="320" height="565" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Gabriel Stedman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carib_indian_family_by_John_Gabriel_Stedman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kalinago may have been related to the mainland Caribs, or Kalina, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, but they spoke an unrelated language, Kalinago or Island Carib, which linguists classify as Arawakan rather than Cariban. Early missionary accounts held that Caribs from South America had conquered and displaced the island's earlier inhabitants, the Igneri. Linguistic and archaeological evidence contradicts a mass migration and conquest. Irving Rouse and others suggest that a smaller group from the mainland arrived without displacing the islanders and took on the local language while keeping traditions of a South American origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the time of Spanish contact the Kalinago were among the dominant groups in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, living across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a> and the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a>, including Guadeloupe. Columbus recorded the name Caribe. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taínos</a> told him the Caribs were fierce warriors and cannibals who raided them. By the middle of the 16th century resistance in the Greater Antilles was largely quashed. In the Lesser Antilles the Kalinago kept their independence against the Spanish and later the French and English, helped by the lack of gold and the heavy Spanish casualties.</p><p>In the 17th century they regularly attacked English and French plantations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a>. In 1660 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> signed the Treaty of Saint Charles with the Island Caribs, which left only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a> and Saint Vincent as reserves, but the English ignored it. By 1763 the British had annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, Dominica and Saint Vincent. On Saint Vincent the Kalinago intermarried with free West African captives, forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garifuna">Black Caribs</a>, or Garifuna, who were expelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> in 1797.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Stories of cannibalism come from Spanish accounts, and there is no hard evidence for it. Historians such as Hilary McD. Beckles suggest the stories were an ideological campaign to justify violent expeditions against the Kalinago. The English word cannibal comes from karibna, an Island Carib word that meant person.</p><p>The Kalinago and their descendants live in the Antilles, notably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago-territory">Kalinago Territory</a> on the east coast was granted by the British government in 1903. The people elect their own chief, and the name Carib was officially changed to Kalinago in Dominica in 2015. Canoes are important in their material culture and economy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garifuna">Garifuna</a>, who share common ancestry with the Kalinago, live mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalinago” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christopher Columbus is said to have passed Dominica, in the Lesser Antilles, an island then inhabited by the <b>Kalinago</b>, who had displaced the earlier Arawak settlers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-3">3 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Dominica's rugged terrain had allowed the <b>Kalinago</b> to hold it long after their neighbours fell, and Britain and France agreed in 1748 to leave it neutral.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-dominica">Colony of Dominica</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers fought the <b>Kalinago</b>, who were driven off within a few decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-guadeloupe">Colony of Guadeloupe</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Kalinago</b> were pushed to the east and then expelled by 1660.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-martinique">Colony of Martinique</a></li><li><blockquote>Arriving French settlers shared the island, and in 1626 the two groups massacred the <b>Kalinago</b> inhabitants at Bloody Point.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Colony of Saint Christopher</a></li><li><blockquote>Saint Vincent was largely held by the Garifuna and <b>Kalinago</b> until the Treaty of Paris of 1763 granted it to Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-vincent">Colony of Saint Vincent</a></li></ul></section>
